    "product_id": "da9e8a0b-cddc-4c82-b991-948c94b5f1dd",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 201855\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n1155 AM MST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- The weather pattern well into next week will support overall \n  dry and quiet weather conditions across the bulk of the Desert \n  Southwest.\n\n- Temperatures through early next week will average a few degrees\n  below normal before a likely warming trend during the latter \n  half of next week pushes readings to slightly above normal.\n \n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nUpper level troughing can be seen on RAP40 500mb height analysis and \non GOES mid-level WV imagery. This trough has brought negative \nheight anomalies to the Desert SW which has lead to below to near \nnormal high temperatures, or in the low to mid 70s across the lower \ndeserts. This trough is also quite dry, with PWATs actually falling \nto around 60% of normal, which will lead mostly clear skies and for \nthe overnight lows to fall 3F-5F below normal. Tomorrow morning and \nSunday morning the lows will bottom out in the mid 40's in the lower \ndeserts, and just below freezing in JTNP and far eastern Gila \nCounty. Due to the below freezing temperatures, a Freeze Warning has \nbeen issued for both those areas on Saturday morning, and then just \nfar eastern Gila Sunday morning. The Freeze Warning in far Eastern \nGila County may be extended into early next week, however more \nspread in the temperatures is present, leading to lower confidence \nif temperatures will continue to meet warning criteria early next \nweek. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /MONDAY THROUGH NEXT FRIDAY/...\nLittle is expected to change with the weather pattern for next\nweek as the split flow upper level pattern is likely to keep the\nbulk of the energy and weather systems well to our north. Models\nare still showing a dry shortwave trough skirting through or just\nto the north of the region on Monday, but really all we can\nexpect it to do is reinforce the cooler air over the region. Going\ninto the middle part of next week, ensemble guidance agrees on a\nbit stronger of a ridge moving through the region and this should\nwarm temperatures back to at least normal to a few degrees above \nnormal by Wednesday. Models are still uncertain with the track and\nstrength of a potential Pacific shortwave trough moving through \nor near our region around next Thursday or Friday. A majority of\nthe GEFS members are still more bullish on this trough, while it \nis much less noticeable within the EPS. However, the \npreponderance of all ensemble members would call for a brief \npause in the warming trend before an even larger ridge is likely \nto build into the region by next weekend. Model trends are \ncurrently favoring daytime highs to climb into the mid to upper \n70s late next week or at the latest next weekend. Even if the GEFS\nis correct and we do get a more organized weather system later \nnext week, it is not likely result in much moisture or\nprecipitation.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1730Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \nNo major aviation concerns are expected through the TAF period.\nCurrent light winds are mainly exhibiting a SSE component and may\nbe more light and variable until the early afternoon. By the early\nafternoon winds will develop their typical W component. Winds are\nnot expected to do their typical shift to E overnight tonight and\nwill likely maintain their W component. However, winds will be\nvery light, generally aob 7 kt and may be light and variable to\ncalm at times. FEW-SCT low cloud decks, with bases aoa 5-6 kft\nwill be common through this evening. Skies will become clear\novernight with FEW high cirrus clouds moving in tomorrow\nafternoon. \n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \nNo major aviation concerns are expected during the next 24 hours.\nW/NW winds will be favored at both terminals. Slightly elevated\nwinds (10-15 kt) are expected at KIPL through the day today before\nspeeds drop below 7 kt during the overnight hours. At KBLH, wind\nspeeds will be aob 8 kt through the period. Skies will be mostly\nclear with occasional periods of FEW high cirrus clouds. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER... \nCool and dry conditions will prevail through the weekend under\nmostly clear skies. After MinRHs of 30-45% today, humidities will\ndip to between 20-30% starting Saturday as high pressure briefly \nsettles into the region. Aside from the occasionally breezy winds\nacross across portions of southeast California, expect overall \nlight winds to prevail. Temperatures will remain slightly below \nnormal through the first part of next week.\n\n&&\n\n.HYDROLOGY...\nGila: A Flood Warning remains in effect from Painted Rock Dam \nthrough the Dateland area due to water releases from the dam. \nWater releases remain around 3000 cfs. This will have the \npotential to flood farm fields and low water crossings along the \nriver downstream from the dam.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...Freeze Warning from midnight tonight to 9 AM MST Saturday for \n     AZZ563.\n\n     Freeze Warning from midnight Saturday night to 9 AM MST Sunday \n     for AZZ563.\n\nCA...Freeze Warning from 11 PM this evening to 8 AM PST Saturday for \n     CAZ560.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Ryan\nLONG TERM...Kuhlman \nAVIATION...Berislavich \nFIRE WEATHER...Kuhlman \nHYDROLOGY...Lojero/Benedict\n",
